# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe --bricscad --mode batch ran an hour past its --timeout 1200
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-bricscad-batch-hangs-past-timeout
#+DATE: 2026-11-19

* Symptom

probe:cad-session-state:windows on PF5S26BT (job 18151974874,
2026-11-18, master e93b79d8 / alfe 2.4.30) started

: alfe --no-init --debug --bricscad --mode batch --timeout 1200 --keep-workdir -l <probe>

at 16:16:28 UTC. Nothing more was printed until the runner killed the job
at its 3600 s limit: alfe never returned, 40 minutes after its own READY
timeout should have ended the run. The same row took 10-18 s an hour
before (job 18149419113) and the day after (job 18176797998).

The probe then captured alfe's output with Out-String, shown only when
alfe exits, so where alfe stood is unknown. Since !574 the probe streams
alfe's output line by line: a recurrence will show the last step reached.

* Context

That afternoon the machine's SNCF/EPURE startup took ~600 s (V:\EPUREPLUS
slow or unreachable, see alfe-com-open-before-quiescent), and the previous
row of the previous job had ended AutoCAD by force at its READY timeout.
Not established: whether alfe hung before READY (launch, wait) or after
its timeout (termination of BricsCAD, cleanup, owned-process Finish).

* Acceptance

- Reproduce, or capture the streamed output of a recurrence.
- Whatever BricsCAD does, alfe returns within --timeout plus its bounded
  teardown, with a diagnostic, and the probe row is reported FAIL.
